        {
          "unit_id": "christmas_party",
          "title": "The Christmas Party",
          "description": "Invita, ofrece y di lo que quieres: would like, want y decide + to. Y los reflexivos: myself, yourself, enjoy yourselves.",
          "lessons": [
            {
              "lesson_id": "christmas_party_lesson",
              "title": "would like and verb + to",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "phase": "context",
                  "type": "audio_context",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "The Christmas party",
                    "dialogue": [
                      {
                        "speaker": "Helen",
                        "text": "Thank you all for a fantastic year. I'd like to welcome our newest designer, Lucía. Her work is excellent."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Thank you, Helen. I'm very happy to be here."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Would you like a drink, Lucía?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Yes, I'd like a glass of wine, please."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "So, are you going home for Christmas?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "Yes. I decided to fly on the twentieth. I want to see my family, and I need some sun!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "I'm going to Galway. My mam wants to know everything about Bristol."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Lucía, would you like to have dinner with me after Christmas? Just the two of us."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "I'd love to."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Really? Grand! I'd like to take you to a little Irish restaurant I know."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "And now, do you want to dance?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "I can't dance… but I'd like to try!"
                      }
                    ],
                    "passage": "Friday, the office Christmas party. Harbour Studio is full of lights and music, and Helen is making a short speech."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "discovery",
                  "type": "multiple_choice",
                  "content": {
                    "instructions": "Fíjate en el diálogo y responde.",
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "question": "\"I'd like a glass of wine.\" I'd = …",
                        "options": [
                          "I would",
                          "I had"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"Would you like to have dinner with me?\" This is…",
                        "options": [
                          "an invitation",
                          "an order"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"I decided to fly on the twentieth.\" After decide we use…",
                        "options": [
                          "to + verb",
                          "verb + -ing"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "What does Daniel answer when Lucía asks him to dance?",
                        "options": [
                          "I can't dance… but I'd like to try!",
                          "No, thanks.",
                          "Yes, I love dancing."
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "would_like_verb_to",
                    "title": "would like and verb + to",
                    "explanation": "«Would like» es una forma educada de «want». Va con un nombre o con «to» + verbo:\n- «I'd like a glass of wine.»\n- «I'd like to take you to a restaurant.»\n\n«Would you like…?» sirve para ofrecer e invitar. Respuestas: «Yes, please.», «I'd love to.», «No, thank you.»\n\nOtros verbos también van seguidos de «to» + verbo: «want», «need», «decide», «hope», «try», «learn»:\n- «I decided to fly on the twentieth.»\n\nOjo con la diferencia:\n- «I like dancing.» (me gusta en general)\n- «I'd like to dance.» (me apetece ahora)",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Estructura",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "would like + nombre",
                          "I'd like a glass of wine."
                        ],
                        [
                          "would like + to + verbo",
                          "I'd like to take you to a restaurant."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Would you like…?",
                          "Would you like a drink?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "Responder",
                          "I'd love to. / Yes, please. / No, thank you."
                        ],
                        [
                          "want / need / decide + to",
                          "I decided to fly on the twentieth."
                        ],
                        [
                          "like + -ing (en general)",
                          "I like dancing. ≠ I'd like to dance (ahora)."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I'd like to welcome our newest designer, Lucía.",
                      "Would you like to have dinner with me?",
                      "I'd love to.",
                      "I want to see my family."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "reflexives",
                    "title": "Reflexive pronouns: myself, yourself, ourselves",
                    "explanation": "Los pronombres reflexivos se usan cuando el sujeto y el objeto son la misma persona:\n- «I cut myself.» (me corté)\n- «Enjoy yourselves!» (¡pasadlo bien!)\n\nTambién sirven para decir yo mismo, sin ayuda:\n- «I made the cake myself.»\n- «Lucía did the designs herself.»\n\nExpresiones muy comunes: «Help yourself!» (¡sírvete!), «Enjoy yourself» (pásalo bien), «Look after yourself» (cuídate), «by myself» (solo, sin nadie).\n\nOjo: en inglés muchos verbos que en español son reflexivos no lo son: «I get up» (me levanto), «I feel tired» (me siento cansado), «I wash my hair» (me lavo el pelo).",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Sujeto",
                        "Reflexivo",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I",
                          "myself",
                          "I made it myself."
                        ],
                        [
                          "you",
                          "yourself / yourselves",
                          "Help yourself! / Enjoy yourselves!"
                        ],
                        [
                          "he / she",
                          "himself / herself",
                          "Daniel hurt himself dancing."
                        ],
                        [
                          "it",
                          "itself",
                          "The door closes by itself."
                        ],
                        [
                          "we",
                          "ourselves",
                          "We enjoyed ourselves at the party."
                        ],
                        [
                          "they",
                          "themselves",
                          "They decorated the office themselves."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Help yourself to a drink!",
                      "Enjoy yourselves, everyone. Merry Christmas!",
                      "Helen decorated the office herself.",
                      "Daniel can't dance, but he enjoyed himself."
                    ]
                  }
                },
